Article 42 For the following first-instance civil cases, a People's Court may, pursuant to Article 38, paragraph 1 of the Civil Procedure Law, transfer the case to a lower People's Court for trial before the hearing:
(1) Cases concerning the debtor in bankruptcy proceedings;
(2) Cases involving a large number of parties and inconvenience in litigation;
(3) Other types of cases determined by the Supreme People's Court.
Before transferring a case to a lower People's Court for trial, the People's Court shall report to and obtain approval from its higher People's Court. After the higher People's Court approves, the People's Court shall make a ruling to transfer the case to the lower People's Court for trial.

Article 43 A judicial officer shall recuse himself in any of the following circumstances, and a party has the right to apply for his recusal:
(1) Being a party to the case or a close relative of a party;
(2) He or his close relative has an interest in the case;
(3) Having served as a witness, expert evaluator, defender, litigation agent, or interpreter in the case;
(4) Being a close relative of the litigation agent in the case;
(5) He or his close relative holds shares or equity of a non-listed company that is a party to the case;
(6) Having other interests with the party or litigation agent that may affect the impartial adjudication.

Article 44 A party has the right to apply for the recusal of a judicial officer in any of the following circumstances:
(1) Accepting banquets from or participating in activities paid for by the party or its authorized agent;
(2) Soliciting or accepting property or other benefits from the party or its authorized agent;
(3) Meeting the party or litigation agent in violation of regulations;
(4) Recommending or introducing a litigation agent to the party, or introducing the case to a lawyer or other person for representation;
(5) Borrowing money or property from the party or its authorized agent;
(6) Having other improper conduct that may affect the impartial adjudication.

Article 52 The term "other organizations" as provided in Article 48 of the Civil Procedure Law refers to organizations that are lawfully established, have a certain organizational structure and property, but do not have the status of a legal person, including:
(1) Sole proprietorship enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license;
(2) Partnership enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license;
(3) Chinese-foreign cooperative joint ventures and wholly foreign-owned enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license of China;
(4) Branches and representative offices of social organizations established according to law;
(5) Branches of legal persons that are established according to law and have obtained a business license;
(6) Branches of commercial banks, policy banks, and non-bank financial institutions that are established according to law and have obtained a business license;
(7) Township enterprises and street enterprises that are registered according to law and have obtained a business license;
(8) Other organizations that meet the conditions provided in this Article.

Article 62 In any of the following circumstances, the actor shall be the party:
(1) Where a legal person or any other organization that should have registered but has not registered, and the actor conducts civil activities in the name of such legal person or other organization;
(2) Where the actor conducts civil activities in the name of the principal without authority, beyond the scope of authority, or after the termination of authority, unless the counterparty has reason to believe that the actor has authority;
(3) Where, after the legal person or other organization is terminated according to law, the actor still conducts civil activities in its name.

Article 87 Pursuant to Article 58(2)(iii) of the Civil Procedure Law, where a relevant social organization recommends a citizen to act as a litigation agent, the following conditions shall be satisfied:
(1) the social organization is a non-profit legal person organization established upon registration according to law or exempted from registration according to law;
(2) the principal is a member of the social organization, or the domicile of one party is within the territorial scope of the social organization's activities;
(3) the agency matters fall within the business scope specified in the articles of association of the social organization; and
(4) the recommended citizen is the principal of the social organization or a staff member who has a lawful labor or personnel relationship with the social organization.
A patent agent recommended by the All-China Patent Agents Association may act as a litigation agent in a patent dispute case.

Article 88 In addition to submitting the power of attorney pursuant to Article 59 of the Civil Procedure Law, a litigation agent shall also submit the relevant materials to the People's Court in accordance with the following provisions:
(1) a lawyer shall submit the lawyer's practicing certificate and the certification materials of the law firm;
(2) a grassroots legal service worker shall submit the legal service worker's practicing certificate, the letter of introduction issued by the grassroots legal service office, and the certification materials that one party is located within the said jurisdiction;
(3) a close relative of a party shall submit an identity document and the certification materials showing the close relative relationship with the principal;
(4) an employee of a party shall submit an identity document and the certification materials showing a lawful labor or personnel relationship with the party;
(5) a citizen recommended by the community or the unit where the party is located shall submit an identity document, the recommendation materials, and the certification materials that the party belongs to such community or unit; and
(6) a citizen recommended by a relevant social organization shall submit an identity document and the certification materials that meet the conditions specified in Article 87 of this Interpretation.

Article 93 A party shall not be required to provide evidence to prove the following facts:
(1) laws of nature and theorems, or rules;
(2) facts that are well known to the public;
(3) facts presumed according to legal provisions;
(4) another fact presumed from known facts and rules of daily life experience;
(5) facts confirmed by a legally effective judgment of the People's Court;
(6) facts confirmed by a legally effective award of an arbitration institution; and
(7) facts proved by a valid notarial instrument.
The facts specified in items (2) to (4) of the preceding paragraph shall be excepted if the opposite party has sufficient evidence to rebut them; the facts specified in items (5) to (7) of the preceding paragraph shall be excepted if the opposite party has sufficient evidence to overturn them.

Article 96 The evidence that the People's Court considers necessary for the trial of a case as specified in Article 64(2) of the Civil Procedure Law includes:
(1) evidence involving possible harm to national interests or social public interests;
(2) evidence involving personal status relationships;
(3) evidence involving the litigation prescribed in Article 55 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) evidence where a party may maliciously collude to harm the lawful rights and interests of others; and
(5) evidence involving procedural matters such as adding parties ex officio, suspension of proceedings, termination of proceedings, or withdrawal.
Except as provided in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall investigate and collect evidence upon the application of a party.

Article 111: The circumstances under which it is genuinely difficult to submit the original of documentary evidence as provided in Article 70 of the Civil Procedure Law include the following:
(1) The original of the documentary evidence is lost, destroyed, or damaged;
(2) The original is under the control of the opposing party, which refuses to submit it after being legally notified to do so;
(3) The original is under the control of another person, who has the right not to submit it;
(4) The original is inconvenient to submit due to its length or volume;
(5) The party bearing the burden of proof is unable to obtain the original of the documentary evidence through applying to the People's Court for investigation and collection or through other means.
In the circumstances specified in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall, in light of other evidence and the specific circumstances of the case, examine and determine whether reproductions of the documentary evidence can be used as the basis for determining the facts of the case.

Article 166 After a ruling for preservation measures is made, under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall make a ruling to dissolve the preservation:
(1) The preservation is erroneous;
(2) The applicant withdraws the preservation application;
(3) The applicant's prosecution or litigation claims are dismissed by an effective judgment;
(4) Other circumstances under which the People's Court deems the preservation should be dissolved.
Where preservation measures implemented by registration are dissolved, a notice of assistance in enforcement shall be issued to the registration authority.

Article 170 The emergency circumstances specified in Article 106, item (3) of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) The need to immediately cease the infringement or remove the obstruction;
(2) The need to immediately stop a certain act;
(3) The need to recover insurance claim payments urgently needed for resuming production or business operations;
(4) The need to immediately return social insurance funds or social assistance funds;
(5) The failure to immediately return funds will seriously affect the right holder's living and production or business operations.

Article 176 If a litigation participant or any other person commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may apply Article 110 of the Civil Procedure Law to deal with the matter:
(1) Making recordings, videos, or photographs without permission;
(2) On-site dissemination of trial activities via mobile communication or other means without permission;
(3) Other acts that disrupt court order and obstruct the conduct of trial activities.
In the circumstances specified in the preceding paragraph, the People's Court may impound the equipment used by the litigation participant or other person for recording, video, photography, or disseminating trial activities, and order them to delete the relevant content; if they refuse to delete the content, the People's Court may take necessary means to forcibly delete it.

Article 187 The acts of obstructing judicial officers from performing their duties by violence, threat or other means as provided in Item (5) of Paragraph 1 of Article 111 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) creating a disturbance or remaining in the People's Court without following the dissuasion of judicial officers;
(2) intentionally damaging or forcibly seizing the legal documents or seizure marks of the People's Court;
(3) creating a disturbance or storming the scene of execution of official duties, surrounding or detaining the persons executing or assisting in the execution of official duties;
(4) damaging, forcibly seizing, or detaining case materials, vehicles for execution of official duties, other instruments for execution of official duties, uniforms of officers executing official duties, and certificates for execution of official duties;
(5) obstructing judicial officers from inquiring, sealing up, distraining, freezing, transferring, auctioning or selling off property by violence, threat or other means; and
(6) other acts of obstructing judicial officers from performing their duties by violence, threat or other means.

Article 188 The acts of refusing to perform a judgment or ruling of the People's Court that has taken legal effect as provided in Item (6) of Paragraph 1 of Article 111 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) concealing, transferring, selling off, damaging property, or transferring property without consideration, trading property at a manifestly unreasonable price, abandoning due claims, providing security for others without consideration, etc., after the legal document has taken legal effect, thereby rendering the People's Court unable to enforce;
(2) concealing, transferring, damaging, or disposing of property that has been provided as security to the People's Court without the permission of the People's Court;
(3) consuming in violation of the People's Court's order restricting high consumption;
(4) having the ability to perform but refusing to perform the obligations specified in the effective legal document in accordance with the enforcement notice of the People's Court; and
(5) an individual who is obligated to assist in enforcement, after receiving the notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, refusing to assist in enforcement.

Article 189 Where a participant in proceedings or any other person commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may apply the provisions of Article 111 of the Civil Procedure Law to deal with the matter:
(1) impersonating another person to initiate or participate in litigation;
(2) after a witness signs an undertaking, giving false testimony, thereby obstructing the People's Court from trying the case;
(3) forging, concealing, destroying, or refusing to produce material evidence concerning the ability to perform of the person subject to enforcement, thereby obstructing the People's Court from ascertaining the property status of such person;
(4) unauthorizedly unfreezing property that has been frozen by the People's Court; or
(5) after receiving the notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, tipping off the party and assisting the party in transferring or concealing property.

Article 192 Where a relevant entity, after receiving a notice of assistance in enforcement from the People's Court, commits any of the following acts, the People's Court may apply the provisions of Article 114 of the Civil Procedure Law to deal with the matter:
(1) allowing the person subject to enforcement to engage in high consumption;
(2) allowing the person subject to enforcement to leave the country;
(3) refusing to stop handling procedures such as the transfer of certificates of property rights, registration of changes of title, and planning approval; or
(4) delaying the handling on the grounds of needing internal consultation, internal approval, or internal regulations.

Article 216 Before the first hearing in the People's Court, where the defendant raises an objection to the acceptance of a civil case on the ground that a written arbitration agreement exists, the People's Court shall review the matter.
Upon review, if the case falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall rule to dismiss the prosecution:
(1) The arbitration institution or the People's Court has already confirmed that the arbitration agreement is valid;
(2) The parties did not raise any objection to the validity of the arbitration agreement before the first hearing of the arbitral tribunal;
(3) The arbitration agreement meets the requirements of Article 16 of the Arbitration Law and does not fall under the circumstances specified in Article 17 of the Arbitration Law.

Article 225 A pre-trial conference may include the following contents based on the specific circumstances of the case:
(1) Clarifying the plaintiff's claims and the defendant's defense opinions;
(2) Examining and handling the parties' applications for adding or changing claims and the counterclaim filed, as well as the claims raised by a third party related to the case;
(3) Deciding upon the parties' applications to investigate and collect evidence, entrust expert evaluation, require the parties to provide evidence, conduct inspection, and carry out evidence preservation;
(4) Organizing evidence exchange;
(5) Summarizing the disputed issues;
(6) Conducting mediation.

Article 252: In a case where the retrial ruling sets aside the original judgment or ruling and remands the case for retrial, if the party applies to change or add claims or files a counterclaim, and any of the following circumstances exists, the People's Court shall grant permission:
(1) the original trial made a default judgment without lawful summons, affecting the party's exercise of litigation rights;
(2) new litigation parties are added;
(3) the subject matter of the action is lost or has changed such that the original claims cannot be realized; or
(4) the claims the party applies to change or add or the counterclaim filed cannot be resolved through a separate action.

Article 255: With respect to an application to inspect a judgment or ruling, the People's Court shall handle it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) if the judgment or ruling has already been disclosed to the public through an information network, the applicant shall be directed to inspect it on their own;
(2) if the judgment or ruling has not been disclosed to the public through an information network, and the application meets the requirements, convenient inspection services shall be provided promptly;
(3) if the judgment or ruling has not yet become legally effective, or has lost legal effect, inspection shall not be provided and the applicant shall be informed;
(4) if the legally effective judgment or ruling was not rendered by this court, the applicant shall be informed to apply for inspection to the People's Court that rendered the effective judgment or ruling; and
(5) if the content requested for inspection involves State secrets, trade secrets, or personal privacy, permission shall not be granted and the applicant shall be informed.

Article 257: The following cases shall not be tried under the summary procedure:
(1) the whereabouts of the defendant are unknown at the time of prosecution;
(2) the case is remanded for retrial;
(3) one party consists of a large number of persons;
(4) the trial supervision procedure is applicable;
(5) the case involves national interests or public interests;
(6) a third party sues to request a change or revocation of an effective judgment, ruling, or mediation agreement; and
(7) other cases that are not suitable for trial under the summary procedure.

第二百六十三条 In a case tried under the summary procedure, the case file shall contain the following materials:
(1) The statement of complaint or the record of oral complaint;
(2) The statement of defense or the record of oral defense;
(3) Documents evidencing the identity of the parties;
(4) The power of attorney or the record of oral entrustment for authorizing another person to act as agent in the action;
(5) Evidence;
(6) Records of questioning the parties;
(7) Records of the trial (including mediation);
(8) Judgment, ruling, mediation document or mediation agreement;
(9) Records of service and pronouncement of judgment;
(10) Status of enforcement;
(11) Receipt for litigation costs;
(12) Where the case is tried in accordance with Article 162 of the Civil Procedure Law, a written notice on the application of the relevant procedure.

第二百七十条 In a case tried under the summary procedure, under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court may appropriately simplify the part concerning the determination of facts or the reasons for adjudication when preparing the judgment, ruling, or mediation document:
(1) The parties have reached a mediation agreement and need to prepare a civil mediation document;
(2) One party clearly admits all or part of the claim of the other party;
(3) In a case involving trade secrets or personal privacy, one party requests the simplification of relevant contents in the adjudicative document, and the People's Court considers the reason to be justified;
(4) Both parties agree to the simplification.

第二百七十四 The following cases involving pecuniary payment shall be tried under the small claims procedure:
(1) Disputes over sales contracts, loan contracts, or lease contracts;
(2) Disputes over alimony, child support, or maintenance where the personal relationship is clear and the dispute exists only over the amount, time, or method of payment;
(3) Disputes over compensation for traffic accident damages and other personal injury compensation where the liability is clear and the dispute exists only over the amount, time, or method of payment;
(4) Disputes over contracts for supply of water, electricity, gas, or heat;
(5) Disputes over bank cards;
(6) Disputes over labor contracts where the labor relationship is clear and the dispute exists only over the amount, time, or method of payment of labor remuneration, work-related injury medical expenses, economic compensation, or compensation;
(7) Disputes over service contracts where the service relationship is clear and the dispute exists only over the amount, time, or method of payment of service remuneration;
(8) Disputes over property management, telecommunications, or other service contracts;
(9) Other disputes over pecuniary payment.

第二百七十五 The following cases shall not be tried under the small claims procedure:
(1) Disputes over personal relationships or confirmation of property rights;
(2) Foreign-related civil disputes;
(3) Intellectual property disputes;
(4) Disputes requiring appraisal or expert evaluation, or where there is an objection to the pre-litigation appraisal or expert evaluation results;
(5) Other disputes that are not suitable for a final instance of first instance.

Article 284 Where an authority or relevant organization prescribed by laws such as the Environmental Protection Law and the Law on the Protection of Consumer Rights and Interests initiates a public interest action against an act that pollutes the environment, infringes upon the lawful rights and interests of numerous consumers, or otherwise harms the public interest in accordance with Article 55 of the Civil Procedure Law, the People's Court shall accept the case if the following conditions are met:
(1) There is a definite defendant;
(2) There are specific claims;
(3) There is preliminary evidence that the public interest has been harmed;
(4) The case falls within the scope of civil actions accepted by the People's Court and is subject to the jurisdiction of the accepting People's Court.

Article 292 Where a third party initiates an action to set aside a legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper, the third party shall file the action within six months from the date when it knows or should know that its civil rights and interests have been harmed, with the People's Court that rendered the effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper, and shall provide evidence materials showing the existence of the following circumstances:
(1) It failed to participate in the proceedings for reasons not attributable to itself;
(2) The whole or part of the content of the legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper is erroneous;
(3) The erroneous content of the legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper harms its civil rights and interests.

Article 295 "Failed to participate in the proceedings for reasons not attributable to itself" as provided in paragraph 3 of Article 56 of the Civil Procedure Law refers to the circumstance where the third party was not listed as a party to the effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper and was not at fault or had no obvious fault. It includes:
(1) The third party was unaware of the proceedings and therefore did not participate;
(2) The third party applied to participate but was not granted permission;
(3) The third party knew of the proceedings but was unable to participate due to objective reasons;
(4) The third party failed to participate for other reasons not attributable to itself.

Article 297 The People's Court shall not accept a third-party action to set aside a judgment, ruling, or mediation paper in the following circumstances:
(1) Cases handled through non-contentious procedures such as special procedures, supervision procedure, public summons procedure, or bankruptcy procedure;
(2) Content involving personal relationship in judgments, rulings, or mediation papers regarding marriage invalidity, annulment, or dissolution of marriage;
(3) An effective judgment or ruling of a representative action case filed by a right holder who did not register, as provided in Article 54 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) An effective judgment or ruling of a public interest action filed by a victim of an act harming the public interest, as provided in Article 55 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 300 In response to a third party's request to set aside in whole or in part the content of a legally effective judgment, ruling, or mediation paper, the People's Court, after trial, shall handle the case according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the request is sustained and the claim to confirm the third party's civil rights is fully or partially sustained, the erroneous part of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation paper shall be changed;
(2) If the request is sustained, but the claim to confirm the third party's civil rights in whole or in part is not sustained, or no claim for confirmation of civil rights is made, the erroneous part of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation paper shall be set aside;
(3) If the request is not sustained, the claim shall be dismissed.
A party dissatisfied with the judgment or ruling provided in the preceding paragraph may appeal.
The parts of the original judgment, ruling, or mediation paper that are not changed or set aside shall remain effective.

Article 302 Where the third party's claims are incorporated into the retrial procedure for trial, the following cases shall be treated separately:
(1) Where the case is tried under the first instance procedure, the People's Court shall try the third party's claims together, and the judgment rendered may be appealed;
(2) Where the case is tried under the second instance procedure, the People's Court may mediate; if mediation fails to reach an agreement, it shall rule to set aside the original judgment, ruling, or mediation statement, and remand the case to the first instance court for retrial, and the third party shall be listed during the retrial.

Article 305 For a person not involved in the case to initiate an execution objection lawsuit, in addition to meeting the provisions of Article 119 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the following conditions shall also be fulfilled:
(1) The person not involved in the case's application for execution objection has been rejected by a ruling of the People's Court;
(2) There is a clear claim for excluding the execution of the subject matter of execution, and such claim is not related to the original judgment or ruling;
(3) The lawsuit is initiated within fifteen days from the date of service of the ruling on the execution objection.
The People's Court shall decide whether to accept the case within fifteen days from the date of receipt of the statement of complaint.

Article 306 For the enforcement applicant to initiate an execution objection lawsuit, in addition to meeting the provisions of Article 119 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China, the following conditions shall also be fulfilled:
(1) The People's Court has ruled to suspend execution upon the application for execution objection by the person not involved in the case;
(2) There is a clear claim for continuing execution of the subject matter of execution, and such claim is not related to the original judgment or ruling;
(3) The lawsuit is initiated within fifteen days from the date of service of the ruling on the execution objection.
The People's Court shall decide whether to accept the case within fifteen days from the date of receipt of the statement of complaint.

Article 313 For an execution objection lawsuit initiated by the enforcement applicant, after trial, the People's Court shall treat it separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the person not involved in the case does not have a civil right or interest in the subject matter of execution sufficient to exclude compulsory execution, it shall render a judgment permitting execution of the subject matter of execution;
(2) If the person not involved in the case has a civil right or interest in the subject matter of execution sufficient to exclude compulsory execution, it shall render a judgment rejecting the claims.

Article 319 Where one or some of the necessary co-litigants file an appeal, the following cases shall be treated separately:
(1) If the appeal only challenges the allocation of rights and obligations between the appellant and the counterparty, and does not involve the interests of other co-litigants, the counterparty shall be the respondent, and the same-side parties who did not appeal shall be listed according to their original trial litigation status;
(2) If the appeal only challenges the allocation of rights and obligations among co-litigants, and does not involve the interests of the counterparty, the same-side parties who did not appeal shall be the respondents, and the counterparty shall be listed according to its original trial litigation status;
(3) If the appeal challenges the allocation of rights and obligations between the parties as well as among co-litigants, all other parties who did not appeal shall be respondents.

The following circumstances may be determined as serious violation of statutory procedures as stipulated in Article 170(1)(4) of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) the composition of the adjudicatory body is illegal;
(2) a judicial officer who should have withdrawn did not withdraw;
(3) a person without litigation capacity was not represented by a legal representative in litigation;
(4) a party's right to debate was illegally deprived.

The People's Court of second instance may, in accordance with Article 169 of the Civil Procedure Law, hear the following appeal cases without a court session:
(1) appeals against rulings on non-acceptance, jurisdictional objection, and dismissal of prosecution;
(2) the appeal claims raised by the party are clearly untenable;
(3) the facts determined in the original judgment or ruling are clear, but the law is incorrectly applied;
(4) the original judgment seriously violates statutory procedures and needs to be remanded for retrial.

Article 347 The public notice for locating a missing person shall contain the following:
(1) The respondent shall, within the specified period, report to the accepting court its specific address and contact information; otherwise, the respondent shall be declared missing or dead;
(2) Any person who knows the current living status of the respondent shall, within the period of the public notice, report what he knows to the accepting court.

Article 357 Where a party applies for judicial confirmation of a mediation agreement, the People's Court shall rule not to accept the application in any of the following circumstances:
(1) The matter does not fall within the scope of cases accepted by the People's Court;
(2) The matter is not within the jurisdiction of the People's Court that received the application;
(3) The application is for confirmation of the invalidity, validity, or dissolution of status relationships such as marital relationship, parent-child relationship, or adoption relationship;
(4) The application involves the application of other special procedures, public summons procedures, or bankruptcy procedures for trial;
(5) The content of the mediation agreement involves the affirmation of real rights or intellectual property rights.
Where, after accepting the application, the People's Court discovers any of the above circumstances that would preclude acceptance, it shall rule to reject the party's application.

Article 360 Upon review, if the mediation agreement falls under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall make a ruling to reject the application:
(1) Violating mandatory provisions of laws;
(2) Damaging national interests, public interests, or the lawful rights and interests of others;
(3) Violating public order or good customs;
(4) Violating the principle of voluntariness;
(5) The content is not clear;
(6) Other circumstances where judicial confirmation cannot be carried out.

Article 367 To apply for the realization of a security interest, the following materials shall be submitted:
(1) A written application, which shall state the basic information such as the name or title and contact information of the applicant and the respondent, as well as the specific claim, facts, and reasons;
(2) Materials evidencing the existence of the security interest, including the principal contract, the security contract, the mortgage registration certificate or the certificate of real rights appurtenant, and the document of title for the pledge of rights or the pledge registration certificate, etc.;
(3) Materials evidencing that the conditions for the realization of the security interest have been satisfied;
(4) A description of the current status of the secured property;
(5) Other materials that the People's Court deems necessary to be submitted.

Article 372 After review, the People's Court shall handle the matter in accordance with the following circumstances:
(1) If the parties have no material dispute on the realization of the security interest and the conditions for such realization have been satisfied, it shall rule to permit the auction or sale of the secured property;
(2) If the parties have a material dispute on part of the realization of the security interest, it may rule to permit the auction or sale of the secured property with respect to the undisputed part;
(3) If the parties have a material dispute on the realization of the security interest, it shall rule to reject the application and inform the applicant to initiate a lawsuit with the People's Court.

Article 377 Where a party applies for retrial, it shall submit the following materials:
(1) A written application for retrial, and submit copies in accordance with the number of the respondent and other parties in the original trial;
(2) If the applicant for retrial is a natural person, it shall submit identity documents; if the applicant for retrial is a legal person or any other organization, it shall submit the business license, the organization code certificate, and the certificate of identity of the legal representative or principal responsible person. Where an agent is authorized to make the application, the power of attorney and the identity documents of the agent shall be submitted;
(3) The original trial judgment, ruling, or mediation statement;
(4) The principal evidence reflecting the basic facts of the case and other materials.
The materials specified in subparagraphs (2), (3), and (4) of the preceding paragraph may be copies verified to be identical to the originals.

Article 378 The written application for retrial shall state the following matters:
(1) The basic information of the applicant for retrial, the respondent, and other parties in the original trial;
(2) The name of the original trial People's Court and the case number of the original trial adjudicatory document;
(3) The specific claim for retrial;
(4) The statutory grounds for applying for retrial and the specific facts and reasons.
The written application for retrial shall clearly specify the People's Court to which the application is submitted, and shall be signed, fingerprinted, or sealed by the applicant for retrial.

Article 383 Where a party applies for retrial, under any of the following circumstances, the People's Court shall not accept it:
(1) an application is made again after the application for retrial has been dismissed;
(2) an application is made against a retrial judgment or ruling;
(3) an application is made after the People's Procuratorate has made a decision not to submit a retrial procuratorial suggestion or a protest against the party's application.
For the circumstances specified in Items (1) and (2) of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall inform the party that it may apply to the People's Procuratorate for a retrial procuratorial suggestion or a protest, except for judgments and rulings made upon retrial due to the People's Procuratorate's procuratorial suggestion or protest.

Article 388 Where the applicant for retrial proves that the new evidence submitted meets any of the following circumstances, the reasons for late submission of evidence may be deemed established:
(1) the evidence already existed before the conclusion of the original trial hearing and was discovered after the hearing due to objective reasons;
(2) the evidence was discovered before the conclusion of the original trial hearing but could not be obtained due to objective reasons or could not be provided within the prescribed time limit;
(3) the evidence was formed after the conclusion of the original trial hearing and cannot be used to file a separate action based on it.
Where the evidence submitted by the applicant for retrial was already provided in the original trial, and the original trial People's Court did not organize cross-examination and did not use it as the basis for adjudication, it shall be deemed that the reasons for late submission of evidence are established, unless the original trial People's Court did not adopt it in accordance with Article 65 of the Civil Procedure Law.

Article 390 Under any of the following circumstances, which result in an erroneous judgment or ruling, it shall be found that the original judgment or ruling clearly errs in the application of law as specified in Item (6) of Article 200 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) the law applied is clearly inconsistent with the nature of the case;
(2) the determination of civil liability clearly violates the agreement of the parties or the provisions of the law;
(3) a law that has ceased to have effect or has not yet come into force is applied;
(4) the provisions on the retroactive effect of laws are violated;
(5) the rules on the application of law are violated;
(6) the legislative intent is clearly violated.

Article 391 During the trial of the original instance, under any of the following circumstances, it shall be found that the party's right to debate is deprived as specified in Item (9) of Article 200 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) the party is not allowed to express its opinion in debate;
(2) a hearing should be conducted but is not conducted;
(3) service of the copy of the statement of claim or the copy of the statement of appeal is made in violation of legal provisions, thereby causing the party to be unable to exercise its right to debate;
(4) other circumstances in which the party's right to debate is illegally deprived.

Article 393 The legal instruments as specified in Item (12) of Article 200 of the Civil Procedure Law include:
(1) legally effective judgments, rulings, and mediation agreements;
(2) legally effective arbitration awards;
(3) notarized creditor's documents with compulsory enforcement effect.

Article 402 During the review of a retrial application, if any of the following circumstances exists, a ruling shall be made to terminate the review:
(1) The retrial applicant dies or terminates, and there is no successor of rights and obligations or the successor declares waiver of the retrial application;
(2) In an action for performance, the respondent who bears the obligation of performance dies or terminates, and there is no property available for enforcement and no person who shall assume the obligation;
(3) The parties have reached a settlement agreement and have fully performed it, unless the parties declare in the settlement agreement that they do not waive the right to apply for retrial;
(4) Another person applies for retrial in the name of a party without authorization;
(5) The original trial court or the next higher People's Court has already ruled to retry the case;
(6) There are circumstances as provided in paragraph 1 of Article 383 of this Interpretation.

Article 404 When a People's Court holds a hearing in a retrial case, it shall proceed in accordance with the following circumstances respectively:
(1) For retrial upon party application, the retrial applicant shall first state the retrial request and reasons, after which the respondent shall make a defense and other original trial parties shall express opinions;
(2) For retrial upon protest, the protesting authority shall first read out the protest document, then the party applying for the protest shall make a statement, after which the respondent shall make a defense and other original trial parties shall express opinions;
(3) For retrial ex officio by the People's Court where there is a petitioner, the petitioner shall first state the retrial request and reasons, after which the respondent to the petition shall make a defense and other original trial parties shall express opinions;
(4) For retrial ex officio by the People's Court where there is no petitioner, the original plaintiff or original appellant shall first make a statement, after which other original trial parties shall express opinions.
For the circumstances provided in Items (1) through (3) of the preceding paragraph, the People's Court shall require the parties to clarify their retrial requests.

Article 406 During the retrial proceedings, if any of the following circumstances exists, the retrial procedure may be terminated by ruling:
(1) The retrial applicant withdraws the retrial request during the retrial period and the People's Court permits it;
(2) The retrial applicant, after being summoned by a summons, fails to appear without justifiable reasons, or leaves the court without permission during the trial, and the case is treated as withdrawal of the retrial request;
(3) The People's Procuratorate withdraws its protest;
(4) There are circumstances as provided in Items (1) through (4) of Article 402 of this Interpretation.
Where the People's Court has ruled to retry a case due to a protest filed by the People's Procuratorate, if the party who applied for the protest falls under the circumstances of the preceding paragraph and no national interests, social public interests, or the lawful rights and interests of others are harmed, the People's Court shall rule to terminate the retrial procedure.
After the termination of the retrial procedure, the original effective judgment or ruling for which enforcement was suspended by the People's Court's ruling shall automatically resume enforcement.

Article 416 Where a local People's Procuratorate at any level, upon the application of a party, issues a retrial procuratorial suggestion to the People's Court at the same level concerning an effective judgment or ruling, it shall be accepted if the following conditions are met:
(1) The retrial procuratorial suggestion, the application of the original trial party, and the relevant evidentiary materials have been submitted;
(2) The object of the suggested retrial is a judgment or ruling that may be retried under the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) The retrial procuratorial suggestion specifies that the judgment or ruling has circumstances as provided in paragraph 2 of Article 208 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) The circumstances provided in Items (1) and (2) of paragraph 1 of Article 209 of the Civil Procedure Law are met;
(5) The retrial procuratorial suggestion has been deliberated and decided by the procuratorial committee of the People's Procuratorate.
If the provisions of the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw it; if it is not corrected or withdrawn, the People's Court shall notify the People's Procuratorate in writing that it shall not accept it.

Article 417 Where the People's Procuratorate, upon the application of a party, files a protest against an effective judgment or ruling, and if the following conditions are met, the People's Court shall rule to retry the case within thirty days:
(1) The protest document, the application of the original trial party, and the relevant evidentiary materials have been submitted;
(2) The object of the protest is a judgment or ruling that may be retried under the Civil Procedure Law and this Interpretation;
(3) The protest document specifies that the judgment or ruling has circumstances as provided in paragraph 1 of Article 208 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(4) The circumstances provided in Items (1) and (2) of paragraph 1 of Article 209 of the Civil Procedure Law are met.
If the provisions of the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court may suggest that the People's Procuratorate make corrections or withdraw it; if it is not corrected or withdrawn, the People's Court may rule not to accept it.

Article 429 Where a creditor's application for a payment order meets the following conditions, the Primary People's Court shall accept the application and notify the creditor within five days after receiving the application:
(1) The claim is for payment of money or negotiable instruments such as bills of exchange, promissory notes, checks, stocks, bonds, treasury bonds, transferable certificates of deposit, etc.;
(2) The money or negotiable instruments claimed have matured and the amount is definite, and the facts and evidence on which the claim is based are specified;
(3) The creditor does not have a countervailing obligation;
(4) The debtor is within the territory of China and has not disappeared;
(5) The payment order can be served on the debtor;
(6) The People's Court that receives the application has jurisdiction; and
(7) The creditor has not applied to the People's Court for pre-litigation preservation.
If the conditions in the preceding paragraph are not met, the People's Court shall notify the creditor within five days after receiving the application that the application is not accepted.
When a Primary People's Court accepts a case for a payment order, it shall not be limited by the amount of the creditor's claim.

Article 430 After accepting an application, the People's Court shall review it by a single judge. Upon review, if any of the following circumstances exists, the court shall rule to dismiss the application:
(1) The applicant does not have the capacity as a party;
(2) The evidentiary documents for payment of money or negotiable instruments do not provide for overdue interest, liquidated damages, or compensation, and the creditor insists on claiming interest, liquidated damages, or compensation;
(3) The money or negotiable instruments claimed are illegally obtained; or
(4) The money or negotiable instruments claimed have not yet matured or the amount is not definite.
After accepting an application for a payment order, if the People's Court finds that it does not meet the acceptance conditions provided for in this Interpretation, it shall rule to dismiss the application within fifteen days from the date of acceptance.

Article 437 Upon formal review, if the written objection raised by the debtor falls under any of the following circumstances, the objection shall be deemed sustained, and the court shall rule to terminate the summary procedure for urging debt repayment, and the payment order shall automatically become invalid:
(1) A circumstance where the application shall not be accepted as provided for in this Interpretation;
(2) A circumstance where the application shall be dismissed by ruling as provided for in this Interpretation;
(3) A circumstance where the summary procedure for urging debt repayment shall be terminated by ruling as provided for in this Interpretation; or
(4) The People's Court has reasonable doubt as to whether the conditions for issuing a payment order are satisfied.

The announcement of acceptance of an application issued by the People's Court in accordance with Article 219 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China shall state the following:
(1) the name of the applicant for public summons for exhortation;
(2) the type, number, face amount, drawer, endorser, holder, payment deadline and other matters of the instrument, as well as the type, number, scope of rights, right holder, obligor, exercise date and other matters of other instruments of rights for which an application for public summons for exhortation may be filed;
(3) the time period for claiming rights;
(4) the legal consequences if the interested party fails to claim rights when the instrument or other instruments of rights are transferred during the period of public summons for exhortation.

The justifiable reasons provided in Article 223 of the Civil Procedure Law of the People's Republic of China shall include:
(1) the interested party is unable to know the facts of the announcement due to the occurrence of an accident or force majeure;
(2) the interested party is unable to know the facts of the announcement due to being restricted in personal freedom, or although aware of the facts of the announcement, is unable to claim rights by itself or through an authorized agent;
(3) the situation does not fall within the statutory circumstances for applying for public summons for exhortation;
(4) the announcement is not made or is not made in the statutory manner;
(5) other objective reasons that cause the interested party to fail to claim rights before the People's Court before the judgment is rendered.

Article 480 Under any of the following circumstances, a notarized creditor's rights document may be determined to be definitely erroneous as specified in paragraph 2 of Article 238 of the Civil Procedure Law:
(1) The notarized creditor's rights document is a creditor's rights document that shall not be given enforcement effect;
(2) The party against whom enforcement is sought does not appear in person or entrust an agent to appear for notarization, or there is any other serious violation of the notarization procedures prescribed by law;
(3) The content of the notarized creditor's rights document is inconsistent with the facts or violates the mandatory provisions of the law;
(4) The notarized creditor's rights document does not specify that the party against whom enforcement is sought agrees to accept compulsory enforcement when it fails to perform or fails to fully perform its obligations.
Where the People's Court determines that enforcement of such notarized creditor's rights document violates the public interest, it shall rule not to enforce it.
After the notarized creditor's rights document is ruled not to be enforced, the parties and interested parties in the notarization matters may initiate a lawsuit regarding the creditor's rights dispute.

Article 522 A People's Court may determine a civil case as a foreign-related civil case under any of the following circumstances:
(1) one or both parties are foreigners, stateless persons, foreign enterprises or organizations;
(2) the habitual residence of one or both parties is outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(3) the subject matter is outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(4) the legal facts that give rise to, change, or extinguish civil relations occur outside the territory of the People's Republic of China;
(5) other circumstances under which the case may be determined as a foreign-related civil case.

Article 532 Where a foreign-related civil case concurrently meets the following conditions, the People's Court may rule to dismiss the plaintiff's action and inform the plaintiff to file an action with a more convenient foreign court:
(1) the defendant raises a request that the case should be under the jurisdiction of a more convenient foreign court, or raises a jurisdictional objection;
(2) there is no agreement between the parties choosing the jurisdiction of a People's Court of the People's Republic of China;
(3) the case does not fall within the exclusive jurisdiction of a People's Court of the People's Republic of China;
(4) the case does not involve the interests of the State, citizens, legal persons, or other organizations of the People's Republic of China;
(5) the main facts in dispute in the case do not occur within the territory of the People's Republic of China, and the case does not apply the law of the People's Republic of China, and there are significant difficulties for the People's Court to hear the case in finding facts and applying law;
(6) a foreign court has jurisdiction over the case and it is more convenient for that court to hear the case.
